course: books
lesson: 96
title: Just So Stories, The Butterfly that Stamped, part 1: a wise king, a hungry Animal and a butterfly's boast
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The last story is about Suleiman-bin-Daoud, Solomon the son of David, the wisest king, who understood the talk of beasts, birds, fishes, insects, rocks and trees. His magic ring could call up Djinns, Fairies and even a great angel. Yet he was not proud: once he tried to feed all the animals in the world in one day, and a single Animal from the sea ate it all in three mouthfuls and asked when dinner would be ready. Suleiman was ashamed of showing off.
explain: He had a great many wives, and most of them quarrelled all day, which made him miserable. Only Balkis, his Head Queen, beautiful and nearly as wise as he, never quarrelled; she loved him and was sorry for him. He would not use his ring on the quarrelling Queens, because that would be showing off, so he walked alone in the garden.
explain: One day, after three weeks of quarrels, Balkis begs him to turn the ring and frighten the Queens, but he remembers the Animal from the sea and says no. He goes off under his great camphor-tree, while Balkis follows quietly and hides among the lilies behind it. Two Butterflies flutter by, quarrelling, and he hears one boast that if he stamped his foot, the whole Palace and garden would vanish in a clap of thunder.
explain: Suleiman laughs till the tree shakes and calls the little Butterfly to his finger. Gently he asks why he told such a fib, since his stamping would not bend a blade of grass. «She is my wife,» says the Butterfly, «and she has been quarrelling with me all the morning.» Back with his wife, the Butterfly brags that Suleiman begged him not to stamp, and Suleiman laughs till he cries.
fact: «Suleiman-bin-Daoud» means Solomon, son of David; Kipling says there are 355 stories about him, «but this is not one of them».
fact: Suleiman understood «what the rocks said deep under the earth» and what the trees said «when they rustled in the middle of the morning».
fact: His ring: turned once, Afrits and Djinns came out of the earth; twice, Fairies came down from the sky; three times, «the very great angel Azrael of the Sword» came, dressed as a water-carrier, with the news of the three worlds.
fact: The Animal from the sea was «the smallest of thirty thousand brothers»; it said «do you really call that a dinner? Where I come from we each eat twice as much as that between meals.»
fact: Suleiman: «I gave that dinner to show what a great and rich king I was, and not because I really wanted to be kind to the animals. Now I am ashamed, and it serves me right.»
fact: In the story he has nine hundred and ninety-nine wives besides Balkis, «just to show that he was the King». Having many wives was a custom of some old kings; today in most countries a person may be married to only one person.
fact: Balkis calls him «O my Lord and Light of my Eyes»; he calls her «O my Lady and Delight of my Life». Both use grand, loving names for each other all through the story.
fact: The Butterfly's boast: «if I stamped with my foot all Suleiman-bin-Daoud's Palace and this garden here would immediately vanish in a clap of thunder.»
fact: Suleiman whispers: «Little man, you know that all your stamping wouldn't bend one blade of grass. What made you tell that awful fib to your wife?»
fact: The Butterfly says «you know what wives are like» and «One must keep them in order somehow»; jokes like this, that all wives or all husbands are a certain way, are old ideas and not true of real people.
fact: Back with his wife, the Butterfly says Suleiman «asked me not to stamp, and I promised I wouldn't», which is a cheeky turn-around of what really happened.
fact: Real life: in the Bible, Solomon was a king of Israel about 3,000 years ago, famous for his wisdom; historians are not sure how much of the old stories about him is history. The Queen of Sheba visits him in the Bible; Arabic tradition calls her Bilqis, Kipling's Balkis.
fact: Real life: a butterfly weighs less than a gram, so its stamp really could not bend a blade of grass; butterflies even taste with their feet, which have taste sensors on them.
fact: Real life: the biggest animal from the sea, the blue whale, can eat about 4 tonnes of tiny krill in a day, so a huge sea-eater is not all made up.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «cap» = a lie; «salty» = cross; «I'm dead» = that's so funny.
exercise: Who is Suleiman-bin-Daoud? | Solomon, the son of David | A Djinn from the desert | The Eldest Magician | Balkis's father, the old king of Sheba
exercise: How many stories about Suleiman does Kipling say there are? | Three hundred and fifty-five | Twelve, like the stories in this book | Thirty thousand | Nine hundred and ninety-nine
exercise: What could Suleiman understand? | Beasts, birds, fishes, insects, rocks and trees | Only people, but in every one of the languages of the world | Only birds | Nothing
exercise: What came when he turned his ring once? | Afrits and Djinns out of the earth | Fairies down from the sky in a cloud of dust | The angel Azrael | Nothing at all
exercise: And when he turned it three times? | The great angel Azrael, dressed as a water-carrier | Four huge Djinns, who lifted the whole Palace high into the air | Fairies | The Butterfly
exercise: Why did Suleiman try to feed all the animals in one day? | To show what a great and rich king he was | Because the animals were starving after a long, dry summer in the desert | Balkis asked him to | It was his birthday
exercise: What happened to the great dinner? | One Animal from the sea ate it all | The Djinns and Afrits ate it in the night while Suleiman and Balkis were asleep | It burned | The birds took it
exercise: Who was the Animal, by its own account? | The smallest of thirty thousand brothers | The biggest whale in all the seas, and king of the others | A Djinn | The Butterfly
exercise: How did Suleiman feel afterwards? | Ashamed of showing off | Proud of his great dinner | Angry with the Animal, so he sent the Djinns after it | Hungry for his own dinner
exercise: How many wives does the story say he had besides Balkis? | Nine hundred and ninety-nine | Three hundred and fifty-five | Thirty thousand | Seven
exercise: Who never quarrelled with Suleiman? | Balkis, the Most Beautiful | The Queen of Egypt | All the Queens | Nobody, because every one of them quarrelled with him
exercise: Why won't Suleiman use his ring on the quarrelling Queens? | It would be showing off | The ring is broken in two | Djinns are afraid of Queens | He has lost it in the garden
exercise: What does he do when they quarrel too much? | Walks by himself in the garden | Shuts himself in the treasure-room with the Gold Bars of Balkis | Shouts | Sails away
exercise: What does Balkis ask him to do? | Turn the ring and show he is the great and terrible King | Leave the Palace and go and live in the desert with her alone | Send the Queens home | Feed the animals again
exercise: What does Suleiman remember when he says no? | The Animal from the sea | All the three hundred and fifty-five stories that people tell about him | The Butterfly | The camphor-tree
exercise: Under which tree does Suleiman go? | The great camphor-tree | An orange tree | A palm-tree | A loquat tree
exercise: Where does Balkis hide? | Among the lilies behind the tree | Inside the trunk of the camphor-tree, which was hollow and full of sweet-smelling leaves | In the fountain | On the roof
exercise: What does the Butterfly boast? | Stamping his foot would make the Palace vanish | He could fly to the Moon and back before the sun goes down | He is the King | He is bigger than an elephant
exercise: What does Suleiman do when he hears the boast? | Laughs till the tree shakes | Gets very angry and calls the Djinns to punish the little Butterfly | Ignores it | Runs away
exercise: «Little man, come here.» What does the Butterfly do? | Flies to his hand, frightened | Flies off at once behind a leaf to hide with his wife, too frightened to come | Stamps | Laughs
exercise: Why did the Butterfly say it, he explains? | To quiet his wife, who had quarrelled all morning | To frighten Suleiman out of his own garden and take the Palace for himself | Because it is true | For a bet
exercise: What does the Butterfly tell his wife Suleiman said? | That he asked him not to stamp | That he laughed at him till the camphor-tree shook and called him a little fibber | That he is a fibber | Nothing
exercise: Looking back: which other story had a Djinn, who gave the Camel his hump? | How the Camel Got His Hump | The Crab that Played with the Sea | The Cat that Walked by Himself | The Elephant's Child
exercise: Looking back: which very first story had a sea-animal that ate every fish it could find? | How the Whale Got His Throat | The Crab that Played with the Sea | The Sing-Song of Old Man Kangaroo | How the Alphabet Was Made
test: Ava: «Suleiman fed all the animals and everyone said thanks, no cap.» (cap = a lie) Put it right. | One Animal from the sea ate it all and asked when dinner was ready | Right, every animal said thank you | The Djinns ate it | The dinner was never cooked
test: Why is Suleiman «not proud», though he has a magic ring? | He hardly ever shows off | He has no real power at all, because the ring only works for Balkis | He is shy | Balkis won't let him
test: What lesson did the Animal from the sea teach Suleiman? | Showing off is silly | Never try to feed any animal again, because they are all far too greedy | Always cook more food | Whales are kings of the sea
test: What would happen, Suleiman fears, if he showed off before the Queens? | He might be even more ashamed | They would love him more and stop quarrelling for ever and ever | He would lose his ring | Nothing
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Butterfly's boast is a fib | The Butterfly really can make the Palace vanish, because he is a magician | Balkis quarrels with the King | Suleiman is angry
test: Ruby, salty (= cross): «the Butterfly told his wife the truth.» Put it right. | He bragged that Suleiman asked him not to stamp | Right, he said Suleiman laughed at him | He said nothing | He told her Suleiman was a fibber
test: «You know what wives are like», says the Butterfly. What should we think of that kind of joke? | It's an old idea; people differ | It's always true of every wife and every husband everywhere, as everyone knows | Only butterflies are like that | It's a law
test: Real life: could a butterfly's stamp bend a blade of grass? | No: it weighs under a gram | Yes, if it stamps very hard with all six of its feet at the same time | Only with its strong left foot | Only in a garden after rain
test: Real life: what odd thing can butterflies do with their feet? | Taste | Hear | See | Sing
test: Real life: who was Solomon, according to the Bible? | A wise king of Israel | A magician of Egypt who kept Djinns in bottles and rode on a magic carpet | An emperor of ancient Rome | A sailor who found Sheba
test: Real life: what name does Arabic tradition give the Queen of Sheba? | Bilqis | Teshumai | Balkis-bin-Daoud | Queen Azrael
test: Looking back: like the Butterfly, which character in the Kangaroo tale was proud and asked to be made special? | Old Man Kangaroo | Yellow-Dog Dingo | Nqong | Nqa
test: Looking back: in the Cat story, the Woman said «I knew I was wise, but I did not know I was beautiful». Who in this part is both wise and beautiful? | Balkis | The Butterfly's wife | The Animal | The angel Azrael
test: Looking back: Pau Amma said «I did not know I was so important». Who in this part pretends to be very important? | The Butterfly | Suleiman | Balkis | The Animal
test: Josh: «I'm dead, the Butterfly said Suleiman begged HIM.» (I'm dead = so funny) Why is this so cheeky? | He turned it round to look grand | Because Suleiman really did beg him, on his knees, under the camphor-tree, in front of Balkis | The Butterfly lied to Suleiman | Balkis told him to
test: ★★★ To call the Djinns, then later the Fairies, then later Azrael, how many turns of the ring is that in all? | Six | Three | Five | Nine
test: ★★★ «There are three hundred and fifty-five stories about Suleiman-bin-Daoud; but this is not one of them.» What is Kipling's joke? | He says this story isn't one of the famous ones, because he made it up | There are only 355 | This story is the 356th exactly | He forgot the number
test: ★★★ The Animal said its brothers each eat «twice as much as that between meals». If the dinner was enough for ALL the animals in the world, the Animal's thirty thousand brothers eat what, between meals? | Twice the whole world's dinner, each | Half a dinner | Three mouthfuls | Nothing
exam: Identify the reason for which Suleiman-bin-Daoud declined to employ his ring against the quarrelsome Queens. | He held that so doing would constitute showing off | The ring had lost its power | The Djinns refused to obey | Balkis forbade it
exam: Ascertain the consequence of Suleiman's attempt to feed all the animals of the world in a single day. | The entire meal was consumed by one Animal from the sea in three mouthfuls | Every animal was satisfied | The meal was carried off by Djinns | The animals refused it
exam: It is given that the Butterfly boasted to his wife. Select the response most consistent with the content of the boast. | That his stamping would cause the Palace and garden to vanish in a clap of thunder | That he was the King | That he could understand the rocks | That he possessed a magic ring
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mass of a butterfly? | Less than a single gram, insufficient to bend a blade of grass | Equal to that of an elephant | Approximately one kilogram | Greater than that of a camphor-tree
